# Codex Adversarial Review

Target: branch diff against origin/main
Verdict: needs-attention

Do not ship as fully fixed. Reviewed clean workflow-v3 at 60caf9b against origin/main 40b484b. R-006: incomplete; ordinary directory tracking works, but multiline pipelines still bypass it. R-007: holds for the recorded option/separator forms. R-008: incomplete; sed write bypasses and read-only false blocks remain. R-009: incomplete; single-line pipelines work, multiline continuation fails. R-010: holds; CD_OPT_CLUSTER_RE recognizes supported clusters. R-011: incomplete; equivalent write syntax and attached expressions escape protection. R-012: incomplete end-to-end; the short-option walk handles attached/separate e/f/l values and real clustered i, but the earlier regex overrides that distinction. New findings: one read-only sed regression, detailed below. Conclusions are static inspection; no files edited or builds/tests run.

Findings:
- [high] R-006/R-009: Pipeline continuation loses directory isolation (.claude/hooks/guard-oracle.sh:112-125)
  For `cd tests/golden; printf x |\ncd ../..; printf x > probe.hpp` (with an actual newline after `|`), Bash runs the second cd inside the pipeline, leaving the parent in tests/golden. The splitter inserts an empty statement at the newline and records a semicolon separator. Consequently, the following cd is marked non-pipeline and resets tracked cur to the repository root; the final protected write passes. Single-line pipeline tracking and ordinary non-piped cd tracking work, but this valid multiline equivalent defeats the fix.
  Recommendation: Preserve pending pipeline membership across continuation newlines and empty segments; cover a right-hand cd after a newline, including subsequent relative writes.
- [high] R-008/R-011: Equivalent sed writes still bypass classification or target extraction (.claude/hooks/guard-oracle.sh:204-213)
  From a guarded cwd, `sed -n '1w probe.hpp' /dev/null` evades the regex because w follows a digit; `s/x/y/gw probe.hpp` likewise evades it because w follows g. Both are valid write forms. Separately, `cd tests/golden && sed -n --expression='w probe.hpp' /dev/null` matches the heuristic, but command_arg_targets discards the entire expression token as a flag and checks only /dev/null. Thus even successfully recognized write commands can create or truncate protected files without blocking. The recorded bare-w and plain s///w cases cover only a subset.
  Recommendation: Extract scripts from positional, attached, and separate expression arguments, recognize addressed write commands and substitution flag sequences, and resolve their actual output filenames instead of filtering whole arguments as paths.
- [medium] R-008/R-012: Earlier regex still rejects attached read-only scripts (.claude/hooks/guard-oracle.sh:56-57)
  `sed -eiprobe tests/golden/nesc/case1_dragless_sphere.hpp` remains blocked before sed_is_inplace runs: the Bash regex sees i inside -eiprobe and a subsequent guarded path. GNU sed interprets this as an attached expression containing an insert command, which only prints to stdout. The new cases test the attached form after cd and the direct-path form with a separated expression, missing their combination. The helper's corrected short-option consumption therefore does not fix the complete hook.
  Recommendation: Use the same option-aware sed classification for direct and cwd-relative paths; remove or replace the conflicting preliminary sed regex and cover attached expressions with direct guarded input paths.
- [medium] New finding: Read-only sed address patterns are mistaken for writes (.claude/hooks/guard-oracle.sh:209-213)
  `cd tests/golden && sed -n '/warning/p' nesc/case1_dragless_sphere.hpp` is newly blocked. The heuristic matches w after the opening slash and accepts the following a as its non-space target, although warning is regex text and p is the only command. command_arg_targets then treats the protected input file as a write target. This regresses ordinary read-only inspection, violating R-008's explicit requirement; it is not merely conservative handling of a genuinely write-capable script.
  Recommendation: Distinguish address/regex and literal-text regions from command positions before recognizing w/W; retain read-only cases containing words beginning with w or W.

